-- title:
--   Newform λ-adic representation: non-unipotent inertia at exponent-two primes
-- statement:
--   Fix $M\ge 1$ and a cusp form $g$ of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$ that is a newform, i.e. a normalised eigenform (with $q$-coefficients satisfying $a_1=1$, multiplicativity at coprime arguments and the usual recursions at prime powers) none of whose eigensystems away from $M$ is matched by a normalised eigenform of proper level dividing $M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $\lambda\neq 2$, let $S$ be a finite set of naturals containing $\lambda$ and every prime divisor of $M$, and let $O'$ be a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field in which $\lambda$ lies in the maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb{C}$ from the Hecke algebra of weight $2$ and level $M$ away from $S$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra generated by the $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$, and the $U_q$ for primes $q\mid M$, $q\notin S$) with $\chi_g(T_\ell)=a_\ell(g)$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ outside $S$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi_g$ into $O'$. Assume the residual hypothesis: whenever $O''$ is a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field, finite over $O'$ via an injective local algebra map, and $\rho$ is a rank-two adically continuous Galois representation over $O''$ such that for every prime $\ell\neq\lambda$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit of $A$ and every Frobenius element $\sigma$ at $\ell$ for $A$ one has $\operatorname{charpoly}(\rho(\sigma))=X^2-\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X+\ell$ in $O''$, then the residual representation of $\rho$ (base change to the residue field) is absolutely irreducible and odd, the latter meaning $\det\rho(c)=-1$ for every $c\neq 1$ with $c^2=1$. The conclusion asserts the existence of such an $O''$, finite over $O'$ through an injective local algebra map, and of a rank-two adically continuous Galois representation $\rho$ over $O''$ satisfying the Frobenius characteristic polynomial identity above for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$ (no exclusion of $\lambda$ being needed, as $\lambda\in S$), and, in addition, such that for every prime $q\neq\lambda$ with $\operatorname{ord}_q(M)=2$ the representation $\rho$ is not unipotent on inertia at $q$: it is not the case that $\operatorname{charpoly}(\rho(\sigma))=(X-1)^2$ for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $P$ and every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This provides the $\lambda$-adic representation attached to a weight-two newform together with a local condition at the primes dividing the level exactly to the second power: at such a prime the representation is not an unramified twist of the Steinberg type, so inertia does not act unipotently. It is used in the level-lowering step, where the absence of unipotent inertia at exponent-two primes is converted into the production of a normalised eigenform of smaller level.
